Rules of Exception Law

1. Logic Puzzle, "For every exception there is an exception, except the exception.".

2. How exception theory came to be, "It started with 'For every exception there is and exception.'. Which then logically led to 'except the exception'. Then came a question: how many 'exceptions' does the phrase 'except the exception' refer to? The answer: exactly ONE - THE Exception. This is the singular grounding moment that cannot be otherwise. All other things can have exceptions (can be otherwise), but THE Exception cannot. It was a hard thing to figure out and crack. Then, Exception Theory became what you see. It was all derived from that.

3. P is for Point, it is the substrate. A Point is infinite. An unbound point is infinite. P is bound to D. The ultimate Point is the total set of infinities, Absolute Infinity.

4. D is for Descriptor, a descriptor describes a point. A Descriptor is Finite. There are finite ways to describe a Point. D is bound to P. An unbound Descriptor would be infinite. However, D is always bound to P, and this binding makes D finite - there are finite ways to describe any given Point. The infinite potential of unbound D explains why D can describe any Point across the infinite set of P, but once bound, D becomes finite. Time, space, causality, and physical laws are Descriptors; the framework is prior to spacetime. More D can be found using other D. The ultimate Descriptor is the total set of finite, Absolute Finite.

5. T is for Traverser, it traverses. A Traverser is Indeterminate. A Traverser can traverse any number of (P°D) as it is indeterminate. There is no limit to the number of T bound at a (P°D) because it is indeterminate. Examples are consciousness, entanglement, and gravity. T binds to D. T is limited by the descriptors it binds to, not all T bind to all D due to indeterminacy. T does not bind to P. T binds anywhere in (P°D) as they are bound. T travels P by using D as (P°D) are bound. T is agency. Indeterminacy grants T the capacity to traverse between Points via Descriptors. T chooses within the constraints of available D. The ultimate Traverser is the total set of indeterminate, Absolute Indeterminate.

6. The binding and interaction are the same. Binding/Interaction is inherent and intrinsic. There is only an illusion that Binding/Interaction is weaker or stronger. T does not collapse into a singularity with (P°D) because it is indeterminate. We will call this Mediation (Binding/Interaction). The ° symbol represents Binding/Interaction (Mediation). When you see (P°D), read this as 'Point bound to Descriptor' or 'Point interacting with Descriptor'.

7. The Binding/Interaction of PD or PDT is what makes something Substantiated (Moment/Exception) or Unsubstantiated (Potential). Pure (P°D) is Unsubstantiated. (P°D) with T is Substantiated, this moment is the Exception. This is the equivalent of mathematical nothing, not 0. P and D bond inherently and inseparably. T does not collapse into (P°D) because indeterminacy prevents it. This is not weakness of Mediation — Mediation is always the same. T's mobility is its nature.

8. The Exception is the grounding factor of Exception Theory. Anything and Everything is possible, except the exception. The Exception/Moment is the grounding thing that is impossible as interaction only traverses to other (P°D) when an outside force acts on it. The Exception is always the current substantiated Moment. It is 'impossible' not because it doesn't exist, but because it cannot be otherwise while it IS. Once interaction occurs, T traverses to a different (P°D), creating a new Exception/Moment.

9. 'Something' and its parts, everything and anything, is relational. There is only pure relativism.

10. S is for 'Something'. 'Something' is everything and anything. 'Something' is comprised of PDT. PDT generates 'Something'. Outside of 'Something' is still 'Something'.

11. S = (P°D°T) Mathematics involves all three primitives, not just D. T traversing mathematical structures is why mathematics appears unreasonably effective.

12. (P°D)°T or T°(P°D) as the T placement does not matter due to (P°D) Binding/Interaction. There can never be (D°P) as a Descriptor must describe a Point. There can never be (D°P) because a Descriptor cannot exist before having a Point to describe - logical priority requires P first, then D describes it.

13. "True" Absolute Nothing cannot exist. The moment it can have a name or be described means it can be described and thus it is 'Something'. One can only attach 'Something' to it, this leads to there always being 'Something'.

14. There is grounding or there is prosperity. The Exception is the grounding of reality. All else is prosperity — maximal possibility. From the grounded Moment, all traversable Something flows. This permits gods, magic, anything that isn't Incoherent.

15. E is for Exception. There is one Exception (the maximally described Point with agency), yet many T share it. This is why shared reality exists. It is the maximally described P with agency (T), so one but many.

16. I is for Incoherence. Incoherence coincides with the Exception. They are the prohibited and self-defeating things.

17. M (B/I) is for Mediation (Binding/Interaction).

18. PDT = EIM so 3=3.

19. The Absolutes are actual configurations within Something, not unreachable limits. Pure relationalism permits P within P, D within D, T within T — nested infinitely.

20. Regions that are True Exception or Incoherent are unable to be traversed. The Moment is complete and any interaction traverses to another. Incoherence has no traverser but cannot be traversed to. They would be noticeable for their unnoticeability. They cover both configuration of Substantiated and Unsubstantiated. The Exception (Substantiated): Has T bound to (P°D). Cannot be traversed because the Moment is complete - any interaction moves T to another (P°D). Incoherence (Unsubstantiated): Pure (P°D) configurations that are self-defeating. Has no T and cannot have T bind to it. Cannot be traversed TO.

21. Reading Order Note: Rules 1-7 establish the foundation (P, D, T, and their bindings). Rules 8-14 explain the Exception and Something. Rules 15-20 complete the system with additional elements and prohibitions.


























